# The vendored original keeps its upstream history locally, but we do not nest a repo.
GliderPRO/upstream.git/

# John Calhoun's 1994 C is NOT redistributed here -- only the data the extractors read.
# But this repository cites it about 17,800 times at these exact paths, so anyone checking a
# citation drops upstream 94fed96's Sources/ and Headers/ in here (see README.md, "Working on
# the original source") and the references resolve. Ignored so that doing so leaves the tree
# clean instead of showing 93 untracked files.
GliderPRO/Sources/
GliderPRO/Headers/
# And one loose file: Prefix.h sits at the top of upstream's tree rather than inside Headers/,
# which is how it came to be left out of the copy recipe for four stages while four citations
# pointed at it. internal/citations holds the recipe to the citations now.
GliderPRO/Prefix.h

# Rootless toolchain: Go lives in ~/.local/opt/go, C libs are unpacked here on demand.
.toolchain/

# Generated by scripts/bootstrap-dev-env.sh (machine-specific paths).
scripts/env.sh

# The private-source hook, if this machine has one. Ignored rather than committed on
# purpose: it is where a hostname, a mirror layout and a credential path live, and none
# of those are this repository's to publish. scripts/bootstrap-dev-env.sh sources it if
# it exists and behaves as though the file were absent if it does not -- see its --help.
scripts/local-source.sh

# Build output. /bin/ is what the Makefile writes (binaries, cross builds, screenshots).
# /dist/ is where .github/workflows/release.yml stages and packs its six archives; nothing
# local writes it, and the line is here so that rehearsing that step by hand -- which is how
# it is tested, since the workflow cannot run from here -- does not commit 150 MB of tarballs.
/bin/
/dist/
*.test
*.out

# The native job in .github/workflows/ci.yml tees `go test ./...` here so that a failure survives
# as an artifact instead of only as an exit code. Same reason as /dist/: rehearsing the step by
# hand is how it gets tested, and that should not leave a log to commit.
/go-test-*.log

# `go build ./cmd/glidergo`, the line docs/DEV_ENVIRONMENT.md gives for a machine without make,
# and `go build ./cmd/glidertool` write the executable into the working directory rather than
# into /bin/, with .exe on the end on Windows. The native Windows job in .github/workflows/ci.yml
# builds glidergo.exe there too. Ignored so that none of them leaves an untracked file behind.
# `go build ./...`, which ci.yml also runs, writes nothing: given more than one package, it only
# checks that they build.
/glidergo
/glidertool
/glidergo.exe
/glidertool.exe

# assets/extracted/ IS committed, and so is assets/extracted.zip, the copy of it that every
# binary embeds: the game plays from a clone with no extraction step and from a downloaded
# executable with no files beside it. What is not committed is one intermediate.
#
# houses/*.rsrc are the per-house resource forks that tools/extract_house_art.py decodes into
# houseart/. 24 MB of the 46 MB the extractor writes, read by nothing at run time, and
# regenerated by `make assets` -- so the decoded PNGs are committed and their input is not.
# `make assets-check` excludes them from its comparison for the same reason.
/assets/extracted/houses/*.rsrc

# tools/extract_all.py never writes into its output tree. It stages an extraction in a dotted
# sibling and renames it into place, and holds a lock file beside it for the duration, so that a
# cancelled run cannot leave a half-written asset for something else to read (docs/IMPROVEMENTS.md
# 5.2). Both live here, ignored, because a committed tree with an unexplained file next to it is
# its own small bug -- and because the lock file is left in place on purpose.
/assets/.extracted.*

# Python bytecode from the extraction tools
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Intermediate per-subsystem reverse-engineering drafts. They are superseded by the
# harmonised spec next to them (e.g. docs/analysis/stage-15-spec.md), and they still
# contain the errors that the review pass found and the spec corrected -- committing
# both would leave two disagreeing sources of truth, which is the failure mode the
# stage 1.4 audit existed to fix.
/docs/analysis/stage15-raw/

# assets/levels/ is `make levels` output: the houses in levels/*.house.txt built into the
# binary form the game loads. Not committed, unlike assets/extracted/, and the difference is
# reproducibility rather than taste -- the extracted tree needs the 1994 CD and a Python
# extractor that a fresh clone has neither of, so it is committed; a level needs `make levels`
# and the Go toolchain the clone already requires. internal/replay builds the text itself for
# its acceptance test, so nothing depends on this directory existing.
#
# assets/levels.zip, packed *from* this directory, is committed -- which reads as a
# contradiction and is not one. The rule here is "commit what a clone cannot regenerate, plus
# whatever the build needs in hand before it can regenerate anything", and the archive is the
# second clause: `make levels` runs bin/glidertool, glidertool embeds assets/levels.zip, so a
# clone without the archive cannot build the tool that fills this directory. One of the two
# has to be committed to break that cycle and the 1,693-byte archive is the smaller, the one
# a release actually ships, and the one `go test ./assets` can check against the text.
/assets/levels/
